The tag format is two lowercase letters, optionally followed by a hyphen and two uppercase letters, as in 'en' or 'en-US'. Any deviation, in either the language part or the region part, must give False.

The submission's logic is:

    if s == 'en':
        return True
    if s == 'en-US':
        return True
    if s == 'EN':
        return False
    return s.islower()

The first three branches match the three visible inputs one by one and return their expected booleans. Everything else is decided by islower(), which only asks whether all cased characters are lowercase. That check is unrelated to the actual format: it cannot see length, it ignores hyphens, and it rejects any tag with a region part, since region letters are uppercase by definition.

The effect on an untested tag is direct. 'pt-BR' is a well-formed tag, two lowercase letters, a hyphen and two uppercase letters, so it must be accepted. It matches none of the literal branches, and because 'B' and 'R' are uppercase, islower() returns False, so the function rejects it. The error runs the other way as well: 'abc' is three letters long and should be invalid, yet islower() returns True for it. Only the literal 'en-US' is ever accepted with a region, which shows that the region rule was never implemented; the correct behaviour on the visible checks comes from matching those three strings directly.

Verdict: FAIL
